---
// "Full-Bleed, Screen-Graded" hero — Riff 14 of the 2026-09 dev-services
// pivot exploration (ui-lab/artifacts/dougrosenbergmusic/hero-from-scratch-
// riffs.html), ported in PR #27. Full-bleed photo, broadcast grade
// (scanlines, screen vignette, warmth) and real animated haze, with the
// content block right-of-center, left-aligned, slightly overlapping Doug's
// body.
// Music-first hierarchy pass, 2026-10-25 (docs/music-first-plan-2026-10-25.md,
// step 2; findings in docs/ui-assessment-2026-10-24.md): the headline is
// roughly 1.6x larger so it stops reading as a caption; the tiny all-caps
// eyebrow became Doug's name (the hero never said it); the four drifting
// "now playing" chips and the "listen now" terminal pill were replaced by
// one row of the real record covers - the same four albums and Amazon links
// the chips carried, but visible at rest, on phones, and under reduced
// motion (the chips sat at opacity 0 whenever their animation was off, so
// reduced-motion visitors never saw them). The terminal pill only repeated
// the primary CTA's #listen link. Archived versions: hero-terminal-widget.html
// in ui-lab, and this file's git history before 2026-10-25.
const records = [
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00G9Y8TPE', label: 'Better Than TV', cover: 'images/albums/better-than-tv.jpg' },
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/Goran-Ivanovic-Group/dp/B000HWY64C', label: 'Goran Ivanovic Group', cover: 'images/albums/goran-ivanovic-group.jpg' },
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B006HTEKHM', label: 'Underwater', cover: 'images/albums/underwater.jpg' },
  { href: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00169L616', label: 'Folk Tales', cover: 'images/albums/folk-tales.jpg' },
];
---

<header id="hero" class="hero">
  <img class="hero__photo" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}images/hero.webp`} alt="Doug Rosenberg holding a saxophone" />
  <div class="hero__haze" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="hero__scan" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="hero__screen-vignette" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="hero__content wrap">
    <p class="hero__name">Doug Rosenberg</p>
    <h1 class="hero__title">Jazz saxophone,<br /> composed and<br /> <span class="accent">played in&nbsp;Chicago.</span></h1>

    <div class="hero__ctas">
      <a href="#listen" class="cta cta-primary" data-cta="hero-primary">Listen to the music <span class="cta__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a href="#lessons" class="cta cta-secondary" data-cta="hero-secondary">Book lessons</a>
    </div>

    <ul class="hero__records" aria-label="Recordings">
      {records.map((record) => (
        <li>
          <a class="hero__record" href={record.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}${record.cover}`} alt="" width="64" height="64" />
            <span class="visually-hidden">{record.label} (opens in a new tab)</span>
            <span class="hero__record-label" aria-hidden="true">{record.label}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>
</header>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    /* 100vh on mobile Safari/Chrome is measured against the viewport with
       the address bar collapsed - taller than what's actually visible when
       the bar is showing (the common case on load/scroll-up), so the
       bottom-anchored content block below (padding-bottom, phone
       breakpoint) sat partly below the visible fold, reading as "clipped."
       100dvh tracks the real visible viewport; vh stays first as the
       fallback for browsers without dvh support. */
    min-height: 100vh;
    min-height: 100dvh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .hero__photo {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 14%;
    /* At this crop's aspect ratio the box is wider than the photo, so
       object-fit: cover already stretches it to full width with zero
       horizontal slack left to pan with object-position - only the
       vertical axis has room. A small scale-up manufactures that slack so
       the subsequent translateX can actually move him left, away from
       the content block; object-position's Y value was pulled back from
       20% to 14% to give the scale's symmetric crop room to grow without
       clipping the hat brim. */
    transform: scale(1.08) translateX(-5%);
    filter: sepia(0.18) saturate(1.08) brightness(1.22) contrast(1.06);
  }

  /* the studio haze already baked into hero.webp (right side of the frame)
     is static pixels - this drifts two soft blurred glows over that same
     region so it reads as living smoke instead of a still photo. Tuned
     stronger/faster than a purely decorative hero needs, since this haze
     now shares the frame with the content block instead of being the only
     thing happening in it. */
  .hero__haze {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .hero__haze::before,
  .hero__haze::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(40px);
  }

  .hero__haze::before {
    width: 42vw;
    height: 42vw;
    top: 8%;
    right: 6%;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(224, 121, 79, 0.28), transparent 70%);
    animation: haze-drift-a 13s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .hero__haze::after {
    width: 38vw;
    height: 38vw;
    top: 28%;
    right: 14%;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(82, 209, 166, 0.22), transparent 70%);
    animation: haze-drift-b 15s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes haze-drift-a {
    0%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0) scale(1); }
    50% { transform: translate(-7%, 8%) scale(1.28); }
  }

  @keyframes haze-drift-b {
    0%, 100% { transform: translate(0, 0) scale(1); }
    50% { transform: translate(8%, -6%) scale(0.82); }
  }

  /* the broadcast grade — scanlines and a soft screen-shaped vignette,
     laid over the full photo instead of shrinking it into a frame */
  .hero__scan {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05) 0 1px, transparent 1px 3px
    );
    mix-blend-mode: overlay;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero__screen-vignette {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 18vw 4vw rgba(6, 9, 15, 0.55);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.1) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.25) 55%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.85) 100%),
      radial-gradient(ellipse 62% 58% at 68% 52%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.6), transparent 72%);
  }

  /* content block sits right-of-center, left-aligned, slightly
     overlapping Doug's body rather than hugging the frame's edge. Starts
     a little further left than the old 50% so the larger headline has
     room for three lines without crowding the frame's right edge. */
  .hero__content {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    left: 46%;
    right: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    max-width: none;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* The name, in the display face's italic and the brass accent, instead
     of a tracked all-caps mono kicker: the staff dividers already carry
     that label idiom further down the page. */
  .hero__name {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 1.6vw, 1.45rem);
    color: var(--brass);
    animation: fade-up 0.7s ease-out both;
  }

  .hero__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 5.4vw, 5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin: 0;
    /* smoke-tinted headline, shimmering within the site's own glow
       family rather than a second competing hue */
    background: linear-gradient(100deg, var(--text) 0%, var(--glow) 32%, var(--text) 58%, #9fe8cd 80%, var(--text) 100%);
    background-size: 300% 100%;
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    background-clip: text;
    color: transparent;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    animation: fade-up 0.7s ease-out 0.15s both, smoke-text 16s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .hero__title .accent {
    background: none;
    -webkit-background-clip: initial;
    background-clip: initial;
    color: var(--glow);
    -webkit-text-fill-color: var(--glow);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  @keyframes fade-up {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(14px); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  @keyframes smoke-text {
    0%, 100% { background-position: 0% 50%; }
    50% { background-position: 100% 50%; }
  }

  .hero__ctas {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* The record row: the proof that this is a working musician, in the
     first screen. Covers rather than text chips; each title shows on
     hover/focus under its own cover, and is always in the accessible
     name via the visually-hidden span. */
  .hero__records {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .hero__record {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: clamp(3.25rem, 4.4vw, 4rem);
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    border-radius: 4px;
    outline: 1px solid rgba(233, 237, 245, 0.14);
    outline-offset: -1px;
    transition: transform var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out), outline-color var(--dur-base) ease;
  }

  .hero__record img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: inherit;
  }

  .hero__record-label {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: calc(100% + 0.45rem);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    color: var(--text);
    white-space: nowrap;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity var(--dur-base) ease;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .hero__record:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--brass);
    outline-offset: 2px;
    transform: translateY(-3px);
  }

  .hero__record:focus-visible .hero__record-label {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .hero__record:hover {
      outline-color: var(--brass);
      transform: translateY(-3px);
    }

    .hero__record:hover .hero__record-label {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hero__name,
    .hero__title,
    .hero__haze::before,
    .hero__haze::after {
      animation: none;
    }
    .hero__record {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* tablet: drop the overlap, pull the block into a plain right-hand
     column instead of jumping straight to a full mobile stack.

     A fixed max-width: 460px looked fine at 768px (where it was originally
     checked) but was never re-checked across the rest of this range - at
     the narrow end (641px) that same 460px box is nearly the full viewport
     width, and left-aligned text inside it starts right where Doug is
     (confirmed via a real 641px screenshot, not assumed). min(540px, 62vw)
     (460px/58vw until the 2026-10-25 headline enlargement, which needed
     the room for "played in Chicago." on one line) keeps the cap wherever
     there's room for it and only shrinks on the narrower widths where
     the vw value is the smaller number. Text is right-
     aligned in this range so short lines pull clear of him rather than
     starting at the box's left edge. */
  @media (max-width: 1024px) {
    /* Lighthouse mobile audit: 50/100, traced to ~5.9s of paint/composite
       work under throttled CPU (desktop: 95/100, no such cost - so this is
       purely a lower-end-hardware concern, not a real bug). The haze
       blobs' drift and the title's shimmer are what run continuously here;
       freezing them to their resting frame (not hiding them - the haze
       glow and gradient title still render, just without the continuous
       repaint/recomposite) keeps the atmosphere while cutting the measured
       cost. Desktop keeps the full animated version untouched. */
    .hero__haze::before,
    .hero__haze::after {
      animation: none;
    }
    .hero__title {
      animation: fade-up 0.7s ease-out 0.15s both;
    }
    .hero__content {
      position: relative;
      left: auto;
      right: auto;
      top: auto;
      transform: none;
      /* .hero__content also carries the global .wrap class (max-width:
         1180px; margin: 0 auto - a page-content gutter meant for normal
         in-flow sections). Inside .hero's flex row, that auto margin wins
         over .hero's own justify-content: flex-end (flexbox gives auto
         margins first claim on free space), so the block was actually
         being centered - equal gaps on both sides - not hugging the right
         edge the way every other breakpoint does. Confirmed via computed
         styles at 665x674 (Doug's "weird size" report). Zeroing it here
         restores the actual right-hug. */
      margin: 0;
      max-width: min(540px, 62vw);
      padding-bottom: 0;
      text-align: right;
    }
    .hero__ctas,
    .hero__records {
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }
    .hero__record-label {
      left: auto;
      right: 0;
    }
    .hero__scrim {
      background:
        linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.1) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.2) 60%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.85) 100%),
        linear-gradient(to left, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.82) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.5) 32%, transparent 62%);
    }
  }

  /* Phone: the content block was vertically centered over the full-bleed
     photo like every wider breakpoint, which on a narrow/short viewport
     put the title and CTAs directly over Doug's face instead of
     beside it - unreadable and cluttered (confirmed via screenshot, not
     assumed). Anchoring it to the bottom instead reuses the scrim's
     already-strong bottom darkening, keeps his face fully visible above
     it, and leaves it a plain dark band to sit on regardless of how many
     lines the title wraps to at any given width.

     That fixed the vertical crowding but not the horizontal - .hero's own
     `justify-content: flex-end` is what keeps the content block right-of-
     center (clear of Doug, who sits left-of-center in the photo) at every
     other breakpoint, but `max-width: 100%` here made the block fill the
     entire row, leaving justify-content nothing to push against. Left-
     aligned text in a full-width block then started flush at the hero's
     left edge - directly over his face again (confirmed via a real
     448x487 screenshot, not assumed). Right-aligning the text itself,
     rather than fighting for width, is the fix that survives regardless
     of exact viewport width or how many lines anything wraps to.

     The record row stays on phones: unlike the old chips and terminal
     pill (removed 2026-10-25 after a real screenshot showed them crowding
     the headline), it's one compact line, and it's the only proof of the
     music in the first screen. Titles are hover/focus-only, so they never
     show on touch; the visually-hidden span still names each link. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero__content {
      max-width: 100%;
      align-self: flex-end;
      padding-bottom: 2.25rem;
      text-align: right;
    }
    .hero__records {
      margin-top: 1.5rem;
    }
    .hero__record {
      width: 3rem;
    }
    .hero__scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.05) 0%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.4) 42%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.88) 62%, rgba(10, 14, 23, 0.95) 100%);
    }
  }
</style>
